WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.390
On February 27, Paramount+ treated viewers
to an absolute pile of heartache and devastation

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.710
with the tenth episode of "1883," "This Is
Not Your Heaven."

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.691
Though the episode feels very much like a
season finale, if not a series finale, the

00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:16.810
network has thus far declined to call its
officially-ordered future episodes a "second

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.810
season."

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.140
This has led some to speculate that it's not,
technically, a finale of either kind.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.391
Whichever way you slice it, the immediate
narrative presented to viewers in the series'

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:28.710
first 10 episodes came to a gut-wrenching
conclusion on Sunday, with a scene that parallels

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.730
an equally moving moment in Season 1 of its
predecessor series, "Yellowstone."

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.370
Despite audiences knowing from the series'
very first scene that the formidable Elsa

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.010
Dutton was destined to be struck by an arrow,
her slow demise as a result of her wound in

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.110
Episode 10 still came as a shock.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.050
As Elsa slowly dies, her father James pulls
her down from her horse, drags her over to

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.090
a tree, and cradles his daughter in his lap
as the two share a final exchange.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:54.050
Whether or not fans felt the scene a fitting
end to Elsa's story, it was, as some have

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.850
noted, a fitting match with "Yellowstone's"
recurring themes.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.430
As fans of "Yellowstone" will recall, the
pilot concluded with a similarly heartbreaking

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:03.760
moment between John Dutton and his recently-deceased
eldest son, Lee.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.720
After lowering Lee's corpse off his horse,
the Dutton patriarch drags his body over to

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.990
a tree so he can share a final embrace with
his son, rest awhile, and choose a spot for

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:12.990
his burial.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.400
A small, loud bird interrupts their time together
and serves as a kind of symbolism for the

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.400
themes of the scene.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.250
As one user pointed out on the series' subreddit,
John and his great-grandfather are "both sitting

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.100
against a tree with their deceased children,
both with a bird."

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.890
Other fans were quick to note the similarity,
with one user adding that both scenes involve

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.229
a character saying they want to "rest for
a bit."

00:01:30.229 --> 00:01:34.020
There are some differences, but for the most
part, the scenes are almost perfect replications

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.020
of one another.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.770
By ending the first 10 episodes of "1883,"
a prequel to the events that take place during

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:43.100
"Yellowstone," on the same exact note upon
which the latter essentially begins, the creators

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.081
of the series have once again foregrounded
the cyclical nature of life, death, and the

00:01:47.081 --> 00:01:48.570
struggle that lies in between.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.390
The death of a popular character is usually
enough to send fans up in arms, but since

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.000
Elsa's fate is telegraphed by design, "1883"
viewers have had the chance to brace themselves

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.150
for quite a while.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:01.670
When the inevitable end comes, and the viewer
notices that the show has no intention of

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:05.450
saving the character in some far-fetched fashion,
many viewers found that they actually liked

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.040
the way the show handled her death.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.040
One Twitter user wrote,

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.539
"I love how Elsa didn't just pass away and
that was it.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:12.810
We were able to see her happy and know that
she was OK.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.120
Completely unexpected yet amazing writing.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.980
Death is so final and leaves you empty.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.639
They managed to not leave us, me anyway, with
that."

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.669
Another user found the character and her fate
both realistic and compelling.

00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:25.109
"Why am I so upset about Elsa in 1883?

00:02:25.109 --> 00:02:28.340
I think I would mourn her death like she wasn't
a fictional character!"

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.120
Another user tweeted a theory that Elsa's
death might be the first in a long line of

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.680
tragedies that Lee's death ties into, and
that Yellowstone 1932 will also feature a

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.019
chilling death in the family.

00:02:38.019 --> 00:02:41.450
"John Dutton, in 'Yellowstone', says numerous
times that he made a promise to never sell

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.450
the ranch.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.629
I'm sure that when 1932 airs about John Duttons
father and grandfather we see this "promise"

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:50.499
made from one generation to the next, and
it all starts with Elsa Dutton's death in

00:02:50.499 --> 00:02:51.499
1883."

00:02:51.499 --> 00:02:54.150
"He says it's wrong for one man to own all
this.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.540
He says you should share it with all the people."

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.780
"Yeah?"

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:03.139
Could it be that the Duttons have paid the
price of their ranch in blood?

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:07.359
It's a chilling idea, and it will indeed be
interesting to see whether the upcoming "1932"

00:03:07.359 --> 00:03:08.549
addresses it.

